{"at":"2026-10-04T12:44:35.821Z","toolCall":{"args":{"Arguments":{"conclusion":"The none network driver completely isolates a container's networking stack. Only a loopback device (lo) is created, and it only gets an IPv4 address (127.0.0.1/8); no IPv6 loopback address is configured.","content":"If you want to completely isolate the networking stack of a container, you can use the --network none flag when starting the container. Within the container, only the loopback device is created. No IPv6 loopback address is configured for containers using the none driver.","scope":"Docker none network driver behavior","source":"https://docs.docker.com/engine/network/drivers/none/"},"ServerName":"project_architect","ToolName":"evidence_record","toolAction":"Recording evidence","toolSummary":"Record evidence"},"name":"call_mcp_tool"},"decision":"allow","stepIdx":57,"imagePath":null}
{"at":"2026-10-04T12:44:47.253Z","toolCall":{"args":{"evidenceId":"evidence-d3383bd6-0357-4e18-b15d-3d0ee874483b","fetchedAt":"2026-10-04T12:42:29.269Z","findings":["The `--network none` flag completely isolates the networking stack of a container.","Within the container using the none driver, only the loopback device (lo) is created.","No IPv6 loopback address is configured for containers using the none driver (only IPv4 127.0.0.1/8 is set)."],"limitations":["Research is limited to official Docker documentation; actual OS-level namespace implementation details (netns) are not covered.","The tool extracted plain text without full semantic HTML structure."],"readOffsets":[0,2000,4000,6000,8000,10000,12000,14000,16000,18000,20000,22000,24000],"scope":"Docker none network driver behavior","sha256":"b64367a2410cd0e1c14ab91bf545de54e38b3b704d320c9a67644fa7a962506b","source":"https://docs.docker.com/engine/network/drivers/none/","toolAction":"Completing task","toolSummary":"Task complete"},"name":"finish"},"decision":"allow","stepIdx":59,"imagePath":null}
